 // This wrapped connection has different behavior than ordinary connection:
 // 1. ReadFrom will return target addr of shadowsocks instead of remote addr
+//
+// NEX (nex-ssu.patch): the client side of this conn never delivered a single reply
+// for the classic AEAD methods (aes-*-gcm, chacha20-ietf-poly1305), so UDP through
+// an "ssu" connector was one-way only. Two bugs, both fixed here:
+//
+//  1. The session was stored and looked up under two different keys. WriteTo
+//     stored it under session.Hash(), which for classic AEAD is "aead-<local addr>"
+//     (go-shadowsocks2 shadowaead/session.go), while ReadFrom looked it up with
+//     core.SessionHashFromAddrPort(<local addr>) = "<local addr>". The lookup always
+//     missed, ReadFrom returned "udp session cannot find" on the first reply and the
+//     relay tore the flow down. The connector-wide map also gained one entry per
+//     flow and was never pruned. A UDPConn is exactly one flow (one dialed socket,
+//     one target), so the session now lives on the conn and the shared map is no
+//     longer touched.
+//
+//  2. A classic AEAD reply is [salt][AEAD(SOCKS addr + payload)], and shadowaead's
+//     ClientHandleOutbound returns the decrypted body unchanged. Standard servers
+//     (sing-box, shadowsocks-rust/libev, Xray) always put the source address first,
+//     so it is stripped here before the payload goes back to the client. SS2022
+//     replies are already stripped by UnpackUDP.
+//
+// A reply that fails to decrypt or parse is dropped (plain UDP semantics) instead of
+// failing the read: one stray or forged datagram must not kill the whole mapping.
 type UDPConn struct {
 	client     *core.UDPClient
 	server     *core.UDPServer
